// String formatting: Displayable and Debug interfaces, format() function.
//
// format(template, args...) is compiler-known (CM1) — it accepts variadic
// arguments through compiler support, not a general variadic mechanism.
// The compiler parses the template at compile time (CM2), type-checks each
// argument against its placeholder (CM3), and generates specialized
// string-building code per call site (CM5).

// ─── Displayable ────────────────────────────────────────────────

/// User-facing rendering. Structs must opt in via
/// `Type implements Displayable`. Primitives implement it by default (D2).
///
/// format("{}", x) calls display() — compile error if not implemented (D4).
///
/// Error bridge (D5): types with message(self) -> string auto-satisfy
/// Displayable — display() delegates to message(). No boilerplate for
/// error types in format("{}", err).
///
/// Rendering is not conversion (D1): `to_string()` is the other verb, for
/// what already holds text (a char, a slice, `StringView`, `Span`,
/// `cstring`), and it isn't part of this interface.
public interface Displayable {
    func display(self) -> string
}

// ─── Debug ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/// Developer-facing representation. Auto-derived for all types (G2).
/// Override via `Type implements Debug` (G3).
///
/// format("{:debug}", x) calls debug() (G4) — the verb matches the interface.
/// Auto-derived output shows struct fields and enum variants.
public interface Debug {
    func debug(self) -> string
}
